# `mentioned_components`

List every component mentioned or clearly described in the transcript.

Each item must contain:

```json
{
  "name": "Event Processor",
  "status": "modified"
}
```

To avoid hallucinations, only mention components explicitly present in the transcript, without
inferring, completing, or inventing names based on context or prior knowledge — a structural
validation checks every `mentioned_components` name against the transcript verbatim.

Allowed statuses:

```text
new
modified
removed
unchanged
unknown
```

If the status cannot be determined reliably:

```json
{
  "name": "Event Processor",
  "status": "unknown"
}
```

In that case, generate a clarification question resolving the status.

---

# `mentioned_data_contracts`

List every data contract identified in Phase 6, one entry per contract — this is the fixed list
the next stage (`data_contract_questions.jinja`) will draft full ODCS-completeness questions
against, so it must be complete and grounded, never invented.

Each item must contain:

```json
{
  "name": "processed-event",
  "producer": "Event Processor",
  "consumer": "Analytics Service",
  "action": "forward-update"
}
```

Allowed `action` values:

```text
new
forward-update
break-change
unchanged
deprecated
removed
unknown
```

Use `forward-update`/`break-change` per Phase 7's compatibility criteria — never a generic
"modified". Use `"unknown"` for `producer`/`consumer`/`action` when the transcript doesn't
establish it — never guess, and generate the corresponding clarification question (Phase
7/Phase 8) instead.

---

# `questions`

This is the complete set of architecture/component/ADR/contract-**identification** questions
required to generate the final architecture-change document, minus full data-contract
specifications (the next stage's job).

Example:

```json
"questions": [
  {
    "id": "adr.rationale",
    "scope": "adr",
    "target": "Architecture Decision",
    "requirement": "rationale",
